<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gamay">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamay is a red wine grape of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the main grape of Beaujolais. In 1395 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> ordered it uprooted from the duchy's vineyards, calling it a 'very bad and disloyal' plant, to protect the reputation of Burgundy wines made from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a>. It thrived further south in the Beaujolais, where its fruity, light wines became famous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamay.jpg" alt="Gamay" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viking59 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gamay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
